A||||5||Steve Normington||3.4 Accessory Brackets||||9||
Z||000000||Steve Normington||04-25-2001||01:52 PM||||What vehicle/year will have brackets that will fit a 3.4 with aluminum heads that the Fiero AC/Compressor/Dogbone will fit?<P>The engine is a FWD from a 97 Silhoutte/Transport/Venture and none of the Fiero brackets will fit. I'd use the van brackets, but they put the alternator on top of the passager's head.<P>BTW, I'm a 18 year old single blonde with a knockout body.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6||||reg||9
Z||000001||Black88GT||04-25-2001||02:07 PM||||Probably have to fab. your own, no one does alum. head swaps (partially for this reason) but its cool to have alum. heads since they flow better.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steve Normington||04-25-2001||02:57 PM||||Not the answer I was looking (praying?) for.<P>(Ben Stein voice) Anyone? Anyone?<P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000003||Lurch||04-25-2001||10:17 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B>Not the answer I was looking (praying?) for.<P>(Ben Stein voice) Anyone? Anyone?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I'm in process of installing 96 Grand Prix 3100 in an 88 coupe. I used alternator bracket and lift hook bracket from 89 lumina/grand prix.  I think they are pretty much the same on most front drive aluminum head 2.8/3.1's.  For A/C mounting, the 3100 with cast alumimum pan uses a compressor that bolts directly to block and pan with 3 long bolts. Top bolts also go through steel dogbone bracket. Get compressor that came with motor, and dogbone bracket, cut excess bracket off and use remainder for spacer between compressor and block. Use hoses and aluminum tubes for new compressor, cut off car end fittings and attach to fiero car side fittings/tubes.  The compressor that came with my 3100 is a v5, but there are no low pressure or high pressure switches on the rear of the compressor, like on fiero 4 cyl v5 compressor. I'm going to use the lo pressure switch on acumulator(from v6 fiero) and haven't figured out what to use for high pressure cut out switch.  Hope this helps.<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Lurch (edited 04-25-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Lurch (edited 04-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tryxalon||04-25-2001||10:33 PM||||<B> I'm in process of installing 96 Grand Prix 3100 in an 88 coupe. </B><P>Why can you not use the Fiero v5 compressor?? <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Lurch||04-26-2001||06:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tryxalon:<BR><B>[b] I'm in process of installing 96 Grand Prix 3100 in an 88 coupe. </B><P>Why can you not use the Fiero v5 compressor?? <P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I thought aluminum dogbone/AC bracket from earlier aluminum head engine would work but the later block isn't drilled for bolt holes required for bracket, and there are no bosses on the block to drill out for the earlier mount.  Maybe there is some other bracket for another application that might mount the old compressor, I don't know.  I just used the new style compressor; bolts on, aligns with other pulleys, etc. Really, the late compressor mounts very cleanly, no mounting brackets at all, it just bolts to the engine.  If I were gathering parts again I would have got the High and low pressure switches from the donor car. Another possibility might be to swap the end housing with switches in it from fiero V5 to newer V5, but the problem with that is that the oil filter is right next to that end of the compressor on the new engine. You wouldn't be able to bolt the fiero hose assembly onto the modified compressor without relocating the oil filter. I just used the new compressor.<BR>||||reg||1
